  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Geography joins the physical earth to the people who live on it. Surface features describe a map, not the subject.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two families are physical geography (the natural environment) and human geography (people and their work).
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Geo + morph + ology: the study of the form of the earth's surface.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Climatology studies the atmosphere and climate; weather at a moment is meteorology.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Population density is a human arrangement. The other three are natural processes.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The biosphere is the living zone where the other three spheres meet.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The stratosphere is a layer of the atmosphere, not one of the four spheres.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A GIS combines layers of data; the satellite is only one source of those layers.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Remote sensing observes the surface from a distance; surveying measures it on the ground.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A rain gauge collects and measures precipitation. The others measure humidity, pressure and wind.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Population geography deals with numbers, growth, distribution and migration.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Economic geography explains where economic activity is located and why.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Weather is momentary; climate is the average over thirty years or more.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A sketch shows relationships; it carries no stated scale, so no distance can be measured from it.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cartography is the making and reading of maps.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fieldwork begins with a question and a plan; measurement follows the plan.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Statistics is a technique used by both branches; the others are content areas.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Geography explains patterns in space — where things are and why.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The comparison of locations and the explanation of a spatial pattern is the geographical question.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The subject bridges the physical environment and human organisation of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrology studies water: rivers, lakes, groundwater and the water cycle.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Soil geography explains how soils form and why they differ from place to place.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Biogeography is the geography of living things and their habitats.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Settlement geography examines the form, site, situation and growth of settlements.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The mean is a statistical measure; it summarises a set of observations.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A photograph records the surface directly; a map interprets it with symbols, scale and grid.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each layer holds one kind of data; combining layers is the system's basic operation.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fieldwork is limited by time and season, which is why repeated visits and sampling are used.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Astronomical telescopes belong to astronomy. Maps, instruments and questionnaires are geographic tools.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The example shows biosphere, lithosphere, hydrosphere and atmosphere changing together.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    25 000 cm = 250 m = 0.25 km.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A map selects, simplifies and scales the surface onto a flat sheet.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The phrase makes people central: the earth is studied as a habitat, not as a rock.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Site selection uses rainfall, relief, soil and drainage — geographic data.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The image is captured by a sensor from a distance, which is the definition of remote sensing.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrology is the study of water. The other pairs are deliberately mismatched.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Discharge = area × velocity; both must be measured, not guessed.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Geography borrows from geology, meteorology, economics and sociology but explains spatial patterns.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Geography requires the explanation of pattern; a list of facts is description only.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A map selects and symbolises; a photograph records everything visible and nothing else.
  ]

